WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.480
So we have just
two compositions,

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.150
both of the compositions
in the second half.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.030
But rather than break
up the flow of those,

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.000
I want to just take
care of something now

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.530
which is very important to do.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:25.350
Although the MIT wind
ensemble is participating

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.750
in the Great Clarinet Summit
along with the Festival Jazz

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.660
Ensemble, this is their
last official concert

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.080
of the academic year.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.610
So I just want to acknowledge
the graduating seniors.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:37.980
We have a few this year.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.080
I'm happy that
there's only a few.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.890
It gets-- it's always sad
when anybody graduates,

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.710
but when there's not--
when there's many, many,

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:50.000
it's even sadder, and
I'm more depressed.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.087
So I'm only halfway
depressed tonight,

00:00:52.087 --> 00:00:53.420
but still depressed nonetheless.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:57.710
In any case, I want
to acknowledge,

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:01.100
and if I could just ask you to
stand up, that would be great.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.440
In the flute section
graduating with degrees

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.420
in electrical engineering
and computer science,

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:06.872
Rachel Thornton.

00:01:06.872 --> 00:01:09.332
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.920
In the oboe section, graduating
with a degree in biology,

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.728
Sue Lee-Anne [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:16.728 --> 00:01:20.600
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.670
In the clarinet
section, graduating

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.800
with a degree in mechanical
engineering, Yasmine

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:25.490
[? Choppins. ?]

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.815
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.210
In the sax-- yes.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:36.350
In the saxophone
section, Ronnie Ogden

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:40.523
with a degree in aeronautics
and astronautics, Ronnie?

00:01:40.523 --> 00:01:44.600
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.880
And Rachel Morgan
also with a degree

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.430
in aeronautics and astronautics

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:53.970
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.830
All of these young
people have made

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.070
vast musical and non musical
contributions to the MIT Wind

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.010
Ensemble, and I thank them all
and will miss them all deeply.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.960
I think Summer's coming
back, because they're

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.580
going to graduate school.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.513
Can we all go to
graduate school here?

00:02:07.513 --> 00:02:10.440
No, we're try to work on that.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.770
At this time of the year, I also
present the Richard and Jody

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.120
Nordlof awards.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.540
This award is presented to a
member or members of the MIT

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.540
Wind Ensemble in recognition
of distinguished service

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.720
and musical contribution
to the MIT Wind Ensemble.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.450
I'm quite sad to report
that Richard Nordlof, MIT

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:32.310
class of 1955 and a long time
and ardent supporter of the MIT

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.340
Wind Ensemble passed
away in January.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.770
We will hold a concert
in his honor next season.

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:42.140
This year there are
three recipients

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.960
of the Nordlof Award.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.770
the first recipient has been the
principal flute and piccolo MIT

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:50.660
WE member for her
entire four years

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:54.100
and a frequent member of the
MIT Festival Jazz assemble.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.060
She's also performed
with the MIT Symphony

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:58.220
Orchestra and
Chamber Music Society

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.430
and other things I
don't even know about.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:05.030
Her dedication, consistency,
and intuitive musicality

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:09.590
is always bright in the MIT Wind
Ensemble overall experience,

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:11.109
Rachel Thornton.

00:03:11.109 --> 00:03:14.103
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.250
Our next recipient has been a
dedicated member for four years

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.980
as well and has held the
soprano saxophone chair

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.980
for all of that time, as well
as playing fantastic alto

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.930
saxophone.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:40.450
Wonderful musician and a
saxophone force of nature,

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.668
you've heard tonight in
both pieces, Ronnie Ogden.

00:03:43.668 --> 00:03:46.656
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:06.755
Final recipient and also a
course sax ensemble member--

00:04:06.755 --> 00:04:08.630
I don't know if you saw
the name of the group

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:12.440
but course six, of course, is,
kind of, ubiquitous major here,

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:16.220
and so the saxophone ensemble
calls himself course sax.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:20.180
And another member is
someone who's anyway I

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:24.410
think of as a very kind of
most calming presence in MIT

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:26.040
student body, one of them.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.400
She's a wonderful musician,
terrific saxophonist,

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.860
and is fiercely
consistent and dedicated

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:34.010
and has served as an MIT
officer for an entire four years

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:35.810
in the social chair role.

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:39.080
She has benignly
and very happily

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.020
forced us all to eat
together, skate together,

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.330
to attend concerts,
and play board games.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:48.260
I think we played to some
interesting board games

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:49.980
for four-year-olds,
which is great.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:51.320
So I'm all for that.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.919
Congratulations
to Rachel Morgan.

00:04:53.919 --> 00:04:56.913
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.980
Lastly, I just want to publicly
thank the entire MIT Wind

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:14.480
Ensemble.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.430
While we're not done yet,
It's been a fantastic year,

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:22.550
and these people do many
amazing things in addition

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:23.420
to making music.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:25.395
And I'm always amazed
when we get together

00:05:25.395 --> 00:05:27.020
and I get to talk
with them one on one.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:29.333
I end up interviewing them
as to what they're doing,

00:05:29.333 --> 00:05:30.500
because it's so fascinating.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:34.185
So I'm just very enamored of
what they do and who they are.

00:05:34.185 --> 00:05:36.060
So thank you all very
much MIT Wind Ensemble.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:43.810
And I do want to do one more
shout out to Ken [? Amos, ?]

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.490
the assistant conductor
of the MIT Wind

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:48.910
Ensemble, and a major force in
what this experience is about.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:50.495
Thank you, Ken, who's up there.

00:05:50.495 --> 00:05:52.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.480
OK, it's my great pleasure
to bring to the stage

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.390
to perform the Byron Concerto
for Clarinet and Wind

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:06.900
Ensemble, a great colleague
and dear friend to MIT WE.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:08.550
But before I do, I
have to acknowledge

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:11.520
that due to an illness of
one of our clarinetists,

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.490
this great colleague I'm about
to introduce after playing

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:18.150
this concerto will also play
our last composition to fill in

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.630
for our ailing clarinetist,
Annette Wu, who we deeply miss

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:24.450
and who is watching, and we
hope for a speedy recovery.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.970
Please welcome, Kenan
Sahin Distinguished

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.830
Professor of Music,
faculty director

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:31.680
of MIT Center for Art,
Science, and Technology,

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.474
and section head of music and
theater arts, Evan Ziporyn.

00:06:36.474 --> 00:06:38.964
[ORCHESTRA MUSIC]

00:24:18.790 --> 00:24:22.060
It's not in your program, but
there'll be a reception right

00:24:22.060 --> 00:24:24.927
after the concert in
the lobby for everybody,

00:24:24.927 --> 00:24:26.760
all the performers and
all audience members.

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:30.980
So please join us, and all
the students and composers

00:24:30.980 --> 00:24:33.540
will be there.

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:37.080
Jamshied Sharifi is
certainly on the short list

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:40.140
of MIT'S most
artistically distinguished

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:45.330
and accomplished alumni as a
composer, arranger, producer.

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:48.600
He's a treasured
colleague and friend

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:51.990
and collaborated with the MIT
Wind Ensemble, the MIT Festival

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:55.230
Jazz Ensemble, and various
projects that sprout about here

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:57.750
on campus, big projects usually.

00:24:57.750 --> 00:24:59.460
We love him very
much, and I'm honored

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:00.840
to invite him to
the stage to say

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:02.850
a few words about
his decomposition

00:25:02.850 --> 00:25:06.150
awakening composed in
recognition of the Arab Spring.

00:25:06.150 --> 00:25:07.695
Please welcome Jamshied Sharifi.

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:34.520
We haven't played really
great [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:37.400
That was a hard piece.

00:25:37.400 --> 00:25:43.600
Probably most of you know it
was Fred's idea for this piece.

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:45.243
And he was drive--

00:25:45.243 --> 00:25:46.660
tell everybody the
story about it.

00:25:46.660 --> 00:25:48.400
You were driving your
car listening to NPR--

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:48.960
Right.

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:49.502
That's right.

00:25:49.502 --> 00:25:51.880
--about the Arab Spring.

00:25:51.880 --> 00:25:54.980
And you thought what a great
[INAUDIBLE] for a piece.

00:25:54.980 --> 00:25:58.880
And I think you emailed me
and told me within a day,

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:01.700
and said so how about this.

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:04.760
And Fred gets
really enthusiastic

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:08.900
about these things,
and his enthusiasm

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:13.400
is so overwhelmingly powerful
that no matter what you

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:16.700
actually think about
it, you say, yeah, sure.

00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:17.200
Let's go.

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:27.120
So I [INAUDIBLE] started
looking into what

00:26:27.120 --> 00:26:29.230
we knew about the Arab
Spring as it unfolded

00:26:29.230 --> 00:26:32.190
and also trying to think
about wind ensemble,

00:26:32.190 --> 00:26:34.350
because I've never
written for it before.

00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:36.850
You find out really quickly
that you have this problem where

00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:39.630
everybody has to breathe,
and so you can't just

00:26:39.630 --> 00:26:43.140
have these really long
notes that go on forever.

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:47.210
But I slog through it and then
started having a lot of fun

00:26:47.210 --> 00:26:47.900
writing it.

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:49.520
Like, it was really enjoyable.

00:26:49.520 --> 00:26:52.340
It was musically interesting.

00:26:52.340 --> 00:26:54.890
The challenge of writing for
wind, and mixing those colors

00:26:54.890 --> 00:26:56.450
was really interesting.

00:26:56.450 --> 00:27:00.350
And it was actually one of
the most joyful things I've

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:06.180
done musically, and it's turned
out to be a lot of fun to hear

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:09.120
and people say to play.

00:27:09.120 --> 00:27:11.880
It's certainly been a great
pleasure for me to come back

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:15.270
here now for the third time and
hear different groups of young

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:16.950
people-- although I 0 there's--

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:23.550
Scott's still there-- different
groups of young people

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:27.300
engaging in the peace and
bringing their own shadings

00:27:27.300 --> 00:27:29.550
and light to it.

00:27:29.550 --> 00:27:31.560
But it really
comes back to Fred.

00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:37.900
His enthusiasm,
his joy for music,

00:27:37.900 --> 00:27:40.770
he's always searching
for new ideas that

00:27:40.770 --> 00:27:42.450
could bring something
to the students

00:27:42.450 --> 00:27:44.100
and to people around him.

00:27:44.100 --> 00:27:47.310
So once again, I thank
him and the ensemble

00:27:47.310 --> 00:27:50.938
for playing this so beautifully.

00:27:50.938 --> 00:27:51.936
Thanks.

00:27:51.936 --> 00:27:53.433
Thank you.

00:27:53.433 --> 00:27:55.928
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:17.884 --> 00:28:20.379
[ORCHESTRA MUSIC]

00:48:09.190 --> 00:48:11.940
[APPLAUSE]

